# ops/Caddyfile — the HTTP policy of the `web` container. Nothing here is TLS.
#
# This file used to open with "the entire public surface of the box". It is not
# that any more, because the box acquired a router of its own. The chain is:
#
#   client → Traefik (terminates TLS, owns 443) → this Caddy → the `api`
#   container → the Node process
#
# and everything below governs the third hop only. Caddy is still here for the
# reason D2-25 gives: this file is not about certificates. It carries a content
# policy derived file by file from what the client actually loads, three classes
# of cache, a deliberate refusal to answer a nonexistent route with the index,
# and a machine-readable 503 that the external monitor consumes. Rewriting that
# in application code would have been the most expensive item of the migration,
# and the least necessary.
#
# NO DOMAIN APPEARS IN THIS FILE, and that is stronger than before rather than
# looser. The site address below is a scheme and a port; the name this deployment
# answers to is assigned in the panel that generates the Traefik router, so the
# repository cannot leak it even by accident (D2-15). Those router labels are NOT
# hand-written here for a second reason: the generator rewrites them from the
# assigned name, and a hand-written set would fight it on the first domain change.
#
# {$VAR} is still substituted BEFORE the file is parsed, and the runtime form
# ({env.VAR}) is still resolved too late to be usable in a site address (P-6).
# The site address no longer carries a placeholder at all, so that trap has no
# target there — but the rule still governs {$DG2_UPSTREAM} below, which is the
# one placeholder left in the file, and it keeps the {$VAR} form for it.
#
# PORT 443 IS NOT CADDY'S ON THIS BOX — it belongs to the Traefik of Coolify,
# shared with another project's production, and this container publishes nothing
# on the host at all. The collision this header used to warn about, TURN over TLS
# wanting 443/tcp, was resolved by giving the relay 3478 (UDP and TCP) and 5349
# (TLS) instead; see ops/turnserver.conf and the relay section of ops/README.md.
# Those two ports cross essentially every home NAT and firewall, which is the
# population this game has: friends on Brazilian residential access.
#
# TURN over TLS on 443 would only buy the corporate firewall that opens nothing
# else, and it is REGISTERED DEBT, NOT BUILT. The two ways out, named here so the
# next reader inherits the options instead of the problem: Caddy's `layer4` app
# routing by ALPN/SNI in front of both services, or a second IP on the VPS.
# Neither is worth its operational weight before somebody is actually locked out
# by a firewall we can name — and under the router above, the first one would now
# have to be arranged alongside Traefik rather than instead of Caddy.
#
# A VERSION CHANGE CLOSES ACTIVE WEBSOCKETS, AND THE OWNER OF THAT CHANGED. It
# used to be `systemctl reload caddy`; it is now the recreation of the container,
# because shipping a version means a new container and not a re-read of this
# file. Every stream through /ws dies with the old container, while the P2P
# DataChannels that never touch Caddy keep running — so the game goes on and the
# signalling server sees everyone leave at once. That is why plan 03-04 puts a
# 60 s grace on room deletion instead of deleting on disconnect: the mitigation
# is the second half of this warning, and removing one without the other brings
# back "everybody dropped at the same time and I did nothing".
#
# WHAT CONTAINERISATION DID NOT TOUCH, listed because the absence of a diff is
# the result and not an oversight. None of it is about where the process runs:
#
#   The whole `header` block. It got MORE important, not less: measured against
#   the neighbouring app on this box on 2026-09-09, the Traefik of Coolify sends
#   none of the four security headers — no HSTS, no nosniff, no Referrer-Policy,
#   no CSP (DM-11). If that block goes, nobody sends them. HSTS still works from
#   behind the proxy because a browser honours it over ITS connection, which is
#   HTTPS with Traefik; the plaintext inner hop does not interfere. AND THE ONE
#   GAP THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO CLAIM IS NOT THERE. It said the error route at the
#   bottom does not inherit those headers. Measured against the image built from
#   this file on 2026-09-10 (plan 02-15, Task 2): with the `api` container
#   stopped, the 503 that `handle_errors` returns carries all four of them.
#   `header` is a SITE-level directive and wraps the response writer before the
#   error chain runs, so the error body is covered like every other. The body is
#   still a fixed generic JSON string, so nothing there was sniffable either way;
#   what the measurement buys is that nobody adds a redundant `header` block
#   inside handle_errors to close a hole that was never open.
#
#   The three cache matchers, including the `not` that makes @assets and @stable
#   mutually exclusive by construction instead of by the order the lines happen
#   to sit in.
#
#   The absence of `try_files {path} /index.html`, which is a decision and not an
#   omission (DM-5). This game has no client-side routing, so a URL that does not
#   exist is a mistake and not a route. Answering it with 200 and HTML would hand
#   the service worker a wrong page to persist in a store that ignores
#   Cache-Control, and the hand-written relative hrefs of index.html would then
#   resolve against the wrong directory. A 404 is the honest answer.
#
#   The `handle_errors` route and its JSON body. With the `api` container down or
#   restarting, the static game keeps serving; what that route adds is a
#   machine-readable signal, so the external monitor of D2-16 can tell "web up,
#   api down" from "box down" — a plain-text "Bad Gateway" looks the same as a
#   timeout to a keyword matcher. There is no HTML maintenance page by decision:
#   the game is static and stays online with the API gone, so a full-page
#   takeover would hide a working game behind a broken one.

# --------------------------------- GLOBAL BLOCK -------------------------------
{
    # The certificate is Traefik's (D2-25). Left on, Caddy would try ACME for a
    # name it does not control and would take 80 and 443 of its own namespace to
    # do it; what a browser would get is a redirect loop. That is a self-inflicted
    # outage on the first deploy, which is why this is the first line of the file
    # (T-2-ACME).
    auto_https off

    # Caddy's administration endpoint listens on localhost:2019 by default and
    # accepts arbitrary configuration reloads from whatever reaches it. Nothing in
    # this deployment uses it, so turning it off is one surface fewer inside the
    # container (T-2-ADMIN).
    admin off

    servers {
        # WITHOUT THE LINE BELOW, THE RATE LIMITER OF PHASE 3 BECOMES ONE BUCKET
        # FOR THE WHOLE INTERNET.
        #
        # Caddy ignores an incoming X-Forwarded-For from an untrusted source — by
        # default and on purpose, because a client can forge one — and REPLACES it
        # with the address it saw itself. Behind a proxy, the address it saw is
        # the proxy's, so every request on earth would reach the Node process
        # wearing one source, and the two outcomes are "nobody is limited" or
        # "everybody is", the second being indistinguishable from the outside from
        # the server being down (DM-10).
        #
        # The real chain is client → Traefik → Caddy → Node, and Traefik already
        # did the honest half: it too refuses a forwarded address from a peer
        # outside its own trusted list, and substitutes the real TCP peer. So what
        # arrives here is correct, and all that was missing was Caddy being
        # willing to believe it. WITHOUT BOTH HALVES THE DEFENCE DOES NOT EXIST,
        # and this was the missing one.
        #
        # The static form of the private ranges is safe here precisely because
        # nothing is published on the host: the only origin that can speak to this
        # port at all is the isolated bridge network Coolify created, and a client
        # on the internet never talks to this Caddy directly. Widening it further
        # would restore forgeability.
        #
        # The consumer is apps/server/src/signaling/limiter.ts, whose clientIp()
        # reads the header this line preserves, and whose own comment warns that a
        # loose setting here would make the value forgeable again. This is the
        # tight form of it (T-2-XFF).
        trusted_proxies static private_ranges
    }
}

# ------------------------------- THE SITE ADDRESS -----------------------------
# A scheme and a port, where a domain placeholder used to be. The explicit
# `http://` is what guarantees plain HTTP: a port-only address would still let
# Caddy choose HTTPS on its own, and this hop is inside the compose network where
# there is nothing to terminate.
#
# The port is 8080, and the `api` container ALSO listens on 8080. That is not a
# conflict, and neither is the 8080 the neighbour's Traefik holds on the host: a
# container's ports live in that container's own network namespace, and nothing
# here is published into the host's. It is the first thing a reader will take for
# a bug, so it is written down rather than discovered.
http://:8080 {
    encode zstd gzip

    # SECURITY HEADERS. Caddy sends NONE of these by default — automatic TLS
    # and HSTS are different things, and only the first one is automatic. And
    # the proxy in front sends none of them either, which the header of this
    # file records as measured rather than assumed (DM-11).
    #
    # At SITE level rather than inside the static `handle`, so that the
    # reverse-proxied /api and /ws answers carry them too. `header` is its own
    # directive with its own place in Caddy's order, so this does not disturb
    # the mutually-exclusive `handle` group below (P-5 stands).
    header {
        # The webroot serves /assets/CREDITS.md and
        # /assets/100_Anims_Order_List.txt right next to the game. nosniff is
        # what stops a browser deciding for itself that one of them is a script.
        X-Content-Type-Options nosniff

        # The path leaks in the Referer otherwise, and phase 6 puts a session
        # cookie on this origin.
        Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin

        # One year, subdomains included, and deliberately WITHOUT `preload`:
        # the preload list is a one-way door — getting off it takes months —
        # and this is one box whose domain may still move.
        Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"

        # DERIVED FROM WHAT THE CODE ACTUALLY LOADS, checked file by file rather
        # than copied from a template:
        #
        #   img-src      src/render/sprites.ts points both sprite sheets at
        #                ${BASE}assets/ — same origin, nothing else.
        #   font-src     the two @font-face rules in src/style.css read
        #                /fonts/*.woff2 — same origin.
        #   connect-src  the API on this origin. 'self' also covers wss:// on
        #                the same host, which is what /ws needs in phase 3.
        #   script-src   index.html carries exactly ONE script element, a module
        #                with a src attribute; grep found no inline script, no
        #                style element, no style= attribute and no data: URI
        #                anywhere in src/, public/ or index.html.
        #
        # No 'unsafe-inline' for style, and that is not an oversight: the game
        # does assign style properties from JavaScript, in src/ui/hud.ts and
        # src/ui/touch.ts, but CSSOM assignment is NOT governed by style-src.
        # Only markup — a style element, or a style attribute in HTML — is.
        #
        # Audio is synthesised through AudioContext (src/app/audio.ts), so there
        # is no media file to allow.
        #
        # OBSERVED IN A RUNNING BROWSER on 2026-09-14, which is what the
        # derivation above had been waiting for since plan 02-03. The session ran
        # against the production domain, with the console open from before the
        # first byte, through a real match to the end of the first wave: nothing
        # was refused by policy, and the four resource families this directive
        # list authorises all loaded — the two spritesheets under the asset root,
        # the two .woff2 faces, and the call to /api/. Audio does not appear in
        # that list because there is no media file to appear: it is synthesised
        # through AudioContext, so the proof of it is hearing the sound.
        #
        # The derivation above STAYS, and the observation proves it rather than
        # replacing it — a directive list nobody can explain is one nobody can
        # safely change. Pasted output, plus what the session does NOT prove, is
        # in docs/OPERACAO.md § "CSP e PWA contra o domínio real".
        Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'"
    }

    # The `handle` blocks below are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE and Caddy sorts them by
    # matcher specificity, so the order written here is for the reader, not for
    # correctness. Rewriting any of them as `route` would make file order
    # load-bearing, and the static block would start swallowing the API the
    # first time somebody moved a paragraph (P-5).
    #
    # THE UPSTREAM IS A SERVICE NAME, resolved by Docker's internal DNS, where it
    # used to be a loopback address. The default written into the placeholder is
    # the name the composition of plan 02-14 gives the Node service, so this file
    # is correct even if nobody set the variable in the panel — and wrong in one
    # loud way, a name that does not resolve, rather than in the quiet way a
    # loopback default would be wrong here.
    handle /api/* {
        reverse_proxy {$DG2_UPSTREAM:api:8080}
    }

    # IN USE by the phase 3 signalling server. It was written a phase early,
    # with no consumer, so the shape would be visible in review — and the bet
    # paid: the block did not change when the server arrived, and it did not
    # change when the deployment became two containers either. Caddy upgrades a
    # WebSocket through reverse_proxy without an extra module, and the CSP above
    # already covers it, because connect-src 'self' includes wss:// on the same
    # origin.
    #
    # What DOES change is the operational rule, and only in who owns it:
    # recreating this container closes every socket here. The header of this file
    # explains why that is survivable and what pays for it.
    handle /ws {
        reverse_proxy {$DG2_UPSTREAM:api:8080}
    }

    handle {
        # INSIDE THE IMAGE, where a release symlink used to be. The bytes the
        # web image carries are the `dist/` that passed the cross-engine gate,
        # copied in at build time, so changing version is changing container —
        # and the atomicity that `rename(2)` used to buy is now the atomicity of
        # bringing the composition up. Nothing swaps underneath a running Caddy
        # any more, which also means nothing has to be resolved per request.
        root * /srv/www

        # Vite emits content-hashed names under /assets: the name changes
        # whenever the bytes change, so pinning them forever is safe, and it is
        # what makes a repeat visit cost nothing.
        @assets path /assets/index-*.js /assets/index-*.css
        header @assets Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"

        # STABLE NAMES, MUTABLE BYTES — the class the two matchers around it
        # left with no Cache-Control at all. /assets/dungeon_tileset.png,
        # /assets/copRobo.png, /fonts/*.woff2 and /icons/*.png are copied from
        # public/ verbatim, so their names do NOT change when their bytes do.
        # With no header the browser applies heuristic freshness, and
        # redeploying changed art under the same filename then serves the old
        # bytes to anyone not yet controlled by a new service worker — for as
        # long as the heuristic says, with no way to dislodge it.
        #
        # Revalidation rather than pinning: one conditional request and a 304.
        # It is not the repeat-visit cost it looks like, because the service
        # worker's per-build cache is what makes a repeat visit free anyway.
        #
        # The `not` is doing real work. Both this matcher and @assets above
        # match /assets/index-<hash>.js, both are `header` directives, and the
        # second one to run would overwrite the first — silently unpinning
        # exactly the files whose whole point is being pinned. Excluding them
        # here makes the two mutually exclusive by construction instead of by
        # the order the lines happen to sit in.
        @stable {
            path /assets/* /fonts/* /icons/*
            not path /assets/index-*.js /assets/index-*.css
        }
        header @stable Cache-Control "public, max-age=0, must-revalidate"

        # The shell has stable names, so it must NEVER be pinned: a stale
        # index.html or sw.js freezes the PWA on an older build and no later
        # deploy can dislodge it. This is the server half of the
        # {cache:'reload'} passed to cache.addAll (P-4) — one half without the
        # other still ships a stale precache.
        @shell path / /index.html /sw.js /manifest.json
        header @shell Cache-Control "no-cache"

        # A 404 stays a 404 here, and that absence is a decision with a reason
        # too long for this line: the header of this file carries it (DM-5).
        file_server
    }

    # The machine-readable signal the external monitor matches on. The reasoning,
    # including why there is no HTML maintenance page, is in the header of this
    # file.
    handle_errors {
        # The body is generic on purpose: no stack trace, no filesystem path and
        # no upstream name reaches the client (T-2-LEAKERR).
        @gateway expression {err.status_code} == 502 || {err.status_code} == 503
        handle @gateway {
            header Content-Type application/json
            header Cache-Control no-store
            respond `{"status":"unavailable"}` 503
        }
        respond "{err.status_code} {err.status_text}" {err.status_code}
    }
}
